{"name":"Parrot Flight","hex":"#018da1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"parrot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A kind of bird, many species of which are colorful and able to mimic human speech, of the order Psittaciformes or (narrowly) of the family Psittacidae.","A parroter; a person who repeats the words or ideas of others."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To repeat (exactly what has just been said) without necessarily showing understanding, in the manner of a parrot."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/parrot"},{"term":"flight","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of flying; the ability to fly.","An instance of flying."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Fast, swift, fleet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flight"}],"entries":[{"title":"Parrot","blocks":[{"text":"Parrots, also known as psittacines from the name of the type genus Psittacus, are birds with a strong curved beak, upright stance, and clawed feet. They are classified in four families that contain roughly 410 species in 101 genera, found mostly in tropical and subtropical regions. The four families are the Psittaculidae, Psittacidae, Cacatuidae (cockatoos), and Strigopidae. One-third of all parrot species are threatened by extinction, with a higher aggregate extinction risk than any other comparable bird group. Parrots have a generally pantropical distribution with several species inhabiting temperate regions as well. The greatest diversity of parrots is in South America and Australasia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Rainbow_lorikeet_%28Trichoglossus_moluccanus_moluccanus%29_Sydney.jpg/330px-Rainbow_lorikeet_%28Trichoglossus_moluccanus_moluccanus%29_Sydney.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parrot","wikidata":"Q31431"},{"title":"Flight","blocks":[{"text":"Flight or flying is the motion of an object through an atmosphere or through the vacuum of space, in this case also called spaceflight, without contacting any planetary surface. This can be achieved by generating aerodynamic lift associated with gliding or propulsive thrust, aerostatically using buoyancy, or by ballistic movement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Juvenile_pelecanus_occidentalis_in_flight.jpg/330px-Juvenile_pelecanus_occidentalis_in_flight.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flight","wikidata":"Q206021"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Parrot_range_2026.png/500px-Parrot_range_2026.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Parrot_range_2026.png/1280px-Parrot_range_2026.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Parrot_range_2026.png/500px-Parrot_range_2026.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParrot_range_2026.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/UCMP_143274.png/500px-UCMP_143274.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/UCMP_143274.png/500px-UCMP_143274.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fossil dentary specimen UCMP 143274 restored as a parrot (left) or an oviraptorosaur","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUCMP_143274.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Cyrilavis_colburnorum_FMNH_specimen.jpg/500px-Cyrilavis_colburnorum_FMNH_specimen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia